there's no delay from the cloud. For example, voice assistants can respond immediately.\n\n3. Data minimization: The phone can process data locally, not sending all data to the cloud, which is better for privacy. Cloud models might have to send more data, increasing risk of data breaches.\n\n4. Enhanced security: By processing data locally"}}, {"text": "", "channels": {"thought": "Okay, let's tackle this query. The user wants me to list 25 distinct things that on-device AI lets a phone do, which a cloud model can't. And each item needs two full sentences explaining why it's possible on-device but not in the cloud.\n\nFirst, I need to recall what on-device AI is. It refers to processing data locally on the phone, not sending it to the cloud. So, the phone handles tasks like image recognition, voice recognition, etc., locally. The cloud model would be running on the server or cloud, not the device.\n\nNow, thinking about the 25 items. Let's start brainstorming. \n\n1. Real-time processing: On-device AI can process data instantly, like translating speech in real time without delays. Cloud models might take longer to process, but the phone can do it locally.\n\n2. Reduced latency: Since the phone is handling it locally, there's no delay from the cloud. For example, voice assistants can respond immediately.\n\n3. Data minimization: The phone can process data locally, not sending all data to the cloud, which is better for privacy. Cloud models might have to send more data, increasing risk of data breaches.\n\n4. Enhanced security: By processing data locally"}}], "versions": {"python": "3.14.8", "platform": "android", "machine": "aarch64", "litert-lm-api": "0.19.0"}, "lib": {"path": "/data/local/tmp/s29q3_0190/kpy/wheel/litert_lm/liblitert-lm.so", "bytes": 22656360, "sha256": "28fa8433febc49a99f4bd3ef8cb82c2912457cfe274d97944debeabdeb97fc78"}, "litert_lm_file": "/data/local/tmp/s29q3_0190/kpy/wheel/litert_lm/__init__.py", "model": "/data/local/tmp/s29q3_0190/W17B.litertlm"